 1079 #uuid "d44a882d-c0e6-5d33-a2fe-067332daaf5d" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413625 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413626 1069 "q-341727" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413626 1071 "Given that $y=(2x+3)^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(2x+3)^{x-1}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413626 1072 #uuid "31fc854a-70c5-5387-9f4b-327eb3d213ff" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413626 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x+3)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x+3)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x+3}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(2x+3)^{x}:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+3)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}) \\\\  & =(2x+3)^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)}{2x+3}+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}) \\\\  & =(2x+3)^{x-1}\\cdot ((2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413624 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413627 1069 "q-138855" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413627 1071 "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413627 1072 #uuid "5a381386-3c00-511f-88a6-9a57fbf09d3b" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413627 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x.$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413623 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413628 1069 "q-341945" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413628 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413628 1072 #uuid "6d2d4364-ca99-526d-979f-b7f7eb9eac46" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413628 1074 "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+1)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2x+1}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y\\,(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=0} & ={(\\sqrt{2(0)+1})}^{(0)^{2}+1}(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)+1)+\\frac{(0)^{2}+1}{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413622 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413629 1069 "q-138812" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413629 1071 "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413629 1072 #uuid "b4ed5979-35bd-5227-b6d2-7a9f08a0cbdf" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413629 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413621 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413630 1069 "q-341953" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413630 1071 "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139534193 17592186045417 true] [17592186413630 1072 #uuid "fed635cb-d378-5d78-b478-1026e862dee7" 1319413953